{"type":"message","id":"fceb2412","parentId":"207b08e8","timestamp":"2026-10-31T18:52:24.118Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the training data.\n\nKey observations:\n- Most decisions: fusedWork < originalWork (fusion reduces work). \n- Example 7 has many decisions where fusedWork > originalWork massively (e.g., 30707584 → 243801600, ~8x growth). These would be rejected by the 2x guard. Note producerElements=12800 small — fusion causes huge work growth (probably broadcast/expanding small producer). These should be rejected.\n- Example 1: many decisions where fusedWork slightly exceeds originalWork (e.g., 16466048 → 16462976? actually less). Some: 7673856 → 7649280 less. Example 3: 7663616→7639040 less. Some are slightly above: example 1 decision \"originalWork\":14455296,\"fusedWork\":14452224 — less. Example 2: 3131136→3100032 less; 3483648→3452544 less; but 3484944→3453840 less. Wait some are slightly above? Look: example 2, \"originalWork\":3583440,\"fusedWork\":3552336 less. Hmm. Example 3: \"originalWork\":7687168,\"fusedWork\":7662592 less. Any with fusedWork > originalWork? Example 7: many. Example 7 decision \"20364800→23385600\" (growth 1.148), \"20211584→23385600\" (1.157), \"11123584→10816384\" (0.972), and the huge ones 8x.\n\nSo decisions with growth >1: mostly producerElements=12800 with fusion expanding into huge work. Also producerElements=1024/128 cases in examples 1,3,4 where fusedWork ≈ originalWork (slightly less, e.g., 0.999). Interesting: those near-parity cases are consumers where producer is tiny (128/1024 elements) — fusion saves a materialization launch but work growth ~1.0. Default threshold 1.02 accepts them.\n\nThe research question: improve convolution implementations while avoiding unnecessary materialization in small computations. The huge-growth cases in example 7 (8x growth with producerElements=12800) are bad — but the hard 2x guard already rejects them. The growth ~1.15 cases (12800 elements) — threshold 1.02 rejects them already.\n\nSmall computations: e.g., example 5 first two: 100352→51200, producerElements=2048 — fusion halves work. Those are good.\n\nWhat could be improved? Cases where fusedWork ≈ originalWork with tiny producer (producerElements 128-1024): fusedWork slightly less than original. These are fusion opportunities that the pointwise decomposition targets — zero-padded 1x1 conv lowered to matmul. Hmm, actually the pointwise step lowers zero-padded 1x1 conv into grouped/batched matmul — this is an exposure decision at program level, not per-consumer.\n\nThe manual program: expose tree threshold 1.02 (aggregate), consumer_tree threshold 2 (accepts any consumer with growth ≤2 — essentially always accepting consumers since hard guard is 2x anyway... consumer_tree threshold 2 means fusedConsumerWork <= consumerWork*2). Cleanup threshold 1.02.\n\nFor the three candidates, vary predicates and thresholds with distinct structure:\n\nCandidate A: Tighter exposure for near-parity cases. Tree: if originalWork > some small threshold (small computations), require stronger saving; for large computations accept threshold 1.02. E.g. tree: {if originalWork lt 1000000, then accept fusedWork/originalWork <= 0.9 (threshold 0.9), else threshold 1.05}. Wait — aggregate threshold accepts fusedWork <= originalWork*threshold; node {\"threshold\": t}. So tree: if originalWork < 1e6 then threshold 0.9 else threshold 1.02. That avoids fusing tiny computations unless big saving. Cleanup: keep threshold 1.02? Vary: cleanup with a tree too — cleanup preserves tiled contraction producers; other producers use cleanup policy. Cleanup threshold maybe 1.05.\n\nCandidate B: use producerElements to distinguish. The bad cases in example 7 had producerElements=12800 (small producer causing huge expansion). Predicate: if producerElements < 50000 then threshold 1.0 (only fuse when work doesn't grow) else threshold 1.02. Actually the huge-growth cases would still be rejected by 2x guard, but the 1.15x cases rejected by threshold 1.0. Hmm but consumer_tree in manual is threshold 2. Candidate B: selective with tree on producerElements and consumer_tree on fusedConsumerWork growth: consumer_tree threshold 1.0 (avoid any consumer-side work growth) — stricter than 2.\n\nCandidate C: region kind — models launch/materialization work. Use region to allow fusing small computations where launch savings dominate: launch_work and materialization_work weights. E.g., region with max_work_growth 1.15, launch_work 4096, materialization_work 4096. Region jointly models launch/materialization; small computations with many launches benefit. Rationale: tiny producers (128/1024 elements) cases where fusedWork ≈ originalWork still save a kernel launch — region policy captures that by charging launch work, allowing fusion at parity. Alternatively a region that avoids materialization: materialization_work high means fusing avoids materialization charge → more permissive.\n\nNote constraint: \"Both stages require explicit policies (not unchanged)\" for pointwise-fusion-sequence; fusion-sequence same grammar with two stages. Use pointwise-fusion-sequence for two candidates and fusion-sequence for one (to omit pointwise rewrite)? The task says \"using the new pointwise-fusion-sequence or fusion-sequence kinds.\" I'll use pointwise-fusion-sequence for two, fusion-sequence for one.\n\nNode budget: 31 total across both trees including one pointwise decomposition node? \"The combined node budget is 31 including one pointwise decomposition node.\" I think my trees just need ≤31 nodes total. Keep trees small (3-7 nodes each).\n\nLet me design three distinct programs:\n\nCandidate 1 \"size-aware-exposure-a\" (pointwise-fusion-sequence):\n- expose: selective, tree: {if originalWork lt 1000000, then {threshold: 0.9}, else {threshold: 1.02}}. Small computations need ≥10% work reduction; large ones default 1.02. consumer_tree: {threshold: 1.02} — symmetric to aggregate rather than the lenient 2.\n- cleanup: threshold 1.02 (same as manual).\n\nCandidate 2 \"producer-scale-guard\" (pointwise-fusion-sequence):\n- expose: selective, tree keyed on producerElements: {if producerElements le 20000, then {threshold: 1.0}, else {threshold: 1.02}}. Rationale: example 7's pathological cases come from small producers (12800 elements) whose fusion expands work >15%; requiring no growth for small producers. Hmm, but example 5 small producers (2048) had 2x savings — threshold 1.0 still accepts. Good. consumer_tree: {if producerElements le 20000, then {threshold: 1.05}, else {threshold: 1.5}} — moderate consumer-side guard.\n- cleanup: tree: {if originalWork lt 500000, then {threshold: 0.95}, else {threshold: 1.02}} — for cleanup, small fused regions must shrink.\n\nCandidate 3 \"region-launch\" (fusion-sequence, omits pointwise rewrite):\n- expose: region: max_work_growth 1.15, launch_work 8192, materialization_work 16. Hmm — need to think about semantics: region weights express estimated work units; selector jointly models launch/materialization. To avoid unnecessary materialization in small computations: charge materialization heavily (materialization_work large → fusing is favored when it avoids materialization). Actually \"Producer materialization remains charged when some consumers still need it.\" Larger materialization_work → more incentive to fuse (fusion avoids materialization). Larger launch_work → fusing (fewer launches) favored. To be permissive on small computations (launch-dominated), set launch_work high. To avoid unnecessary materialization... hmm, \"avoiding unnecessary materialization\" means we want fusion to eliminate materializations → set materialization_work high (charging materialization makes fusion more attractive). But then risk of overfusing. Set max_work_growth 1.15, launch_work 16384, materialization_work 32.\n- cleanup: threshold 1.02.\n\nWait — is region allowed as expose/cleanup policy within a program? Program expose:POLICY, cleanup:POLICY — policies are kiln-fusion-policy, region is one kind. Yes.\n\nAlso cleanup must keep \"recognized contraction producers whose minimum estimated schedule is tiled\" — automatic.\n\nCheck node counts: each tree ≤ 31 total across both trees (tree + consumer_tree combined). All fine.\n\nThresholds must be in [0,2] for max_work_growth. Region: launch_work in [0,1048576], materialization_work in [0,64]. materialization_work max 64.\n\nTree threshold values: aggregate threshold accepts fusedWork <= originalWork*threshold. For \"then\" nodes with threshold 0.9: fine, number.\n\nDepth ≤6, fine.\n\nDistinct IDs lowercase alnum/hyphen.\n\nLet me write JSON strings.\n\nCandidate 1:\n{\n \"format\":\"kiln-transformation-program/1\",\n \"id\":\"sizeaware-exposure-a\",\n \"kind\":\"pointwise-fusion-sequence\",\n \"expose\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id\":\"expose-size-split\",\"kind\":\"selective\",\n   \"tree\":{\"if\":{\"feature\":\"originalWork\",\"op\":\"lt\",\"value\":1000000},\"then\":{\"threshold\":0.9},\"else\":{\"threshold\":1.02}},\n   \"consumer_tree\":{\"threshold\":1.02}},\n \"cleanup\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id\":\"cleanup-std\",\"kind\":\"threshold\",\"max_work_growth\":1.02}\n}\n\nCandidate 2:\n{\n \"format\":\"kiln-transformation-program/1\",\n \"id\":\"producerscale-guard-b\",\n \"kind\":\"pointwise-fusion-sequence\",\n \"expose\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id\":\"expose-producer-scale\",\"kind\":\"selective\",\n   \"tree\":{\"if\":{\"feature\":\"producerElements\",\"op\":\"le\",\"value\":20000},\"then\":{\"threshold\":1.0},\"else\":{\"threshold\":1.02}},\n   \"consumer_tree\":{\"if\":{\"feature\":\"producerElements\",\"op\":\"le\",\"value\":20000},\"then\":{\"threshold\":1.05},\"else\":{\"threshold\":1.5}}},\n \"cleanup\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id\":\"cleanup-size-split\",\"kind\":\"selective\",\n   \"tree\":{\"if\":{\"feature\":\"originalWork\",\"op\":\"lt\",\"value\":500000},\"then\":{\"threshold\":0.95},\"else\":{\"threshold\":1.02}}}\n}\n\nHmm, cleanup selective requires tree and consumer_tree. \"selective\": tree and consumer_tree. So cleanup selective needs both trees. Let me give cleanup selective both trees, or just use threshold. I'll give cleanup as selective with both trees matching:\ncleanup tree: {if originalWork lt 500000 then threshold 0.95 else threshold 1.02}, consumer_tree: {threshold:1.02}.\n\nWait — does consumer_tree have access to producerElements? Consumer features include producerElements. Yes.\n\nCandidate 3:\n{\n \"format\":\"kiln-transformation-program/1\",\n \"id\":\"region-launch-c\",\n \"kind\":\"fusion-sequence\",\n \"expose\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id\":\"expose-region-launch\",\"kind\":\"region\",\n   \"max_work_growth\":1.15,\"launch_work\":16384,\"materialization_work\":32},\n \"cleanup\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id\":\"cleanup-std-c\",\"kind\":\"threshold\",\"max_work_growth\":1.02}\n}\n\nCheck JSON validity mentally. Threshold 1.0 — fine number. All within bounds.\n\nJustification summary in text, then submit. The three calls are independent? Submissions consume slots; they're independent — could submit all in one block. But better to submit sequentially? No dependencies; I can do all three in one block per instructions. I'll do that.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"The training traces support a few concrete observations:\n\n- **Large, real savings are the norm**: most decisions shrink work substantially (e.g. 16685440→10226560; 1819196496→672578640), so the default 1.02 exposure is safely in the profitable regime for big producers.\n- **Near-parity cases are dominated by tiny producers** (producerElements 128–4096): fusedWork ≈ originalWork (ratio ≈ 0.999–1.0). These likely save a materialization/launch rather than scalar work.\n- **Pathological growth clusters around one small-producer size**: in example 7, decisions with producerElements=12800 grow work 1.15x and even ~8–16x (e.g. 30707584→243801600). The 2x hard guard catches the extreme ones, but the ~1.15x cases slip through a 1.02 threshold only if the decision feature is the *right* one — the manual policy's consumer_tree threshold of 2 accepts all of them on the consumer side.\n\nBased on that, three distinct programs varying both predicates and thresholds:\n\n**A — size-split aggregate exposure**: small computations (originalWork < 1e6, e.g. example 5's ~1e5 decisions) must show a real work reduction (≤0.9x) before exposure; large computations keep the default 1.02. Consumer side tightened from 2 to 1.02 so consumer-side growth is no longer rubber-stamped.\n\n**B — producer-scale guard**: predicate on producerElements instead of total work. Small producers (≤20000 elements, which is where all observed growth>1 cases live) must not grow work at all; large producers keep 1.02. Consumer tree uses a split 1.05/1.5 guard. Cleanup also gets a size-split selective policy (small fused regions must actually shrink).\n\n**C — region model, no pointwise rewrite (fusion-sequence)**: jointly charges launch work (16384) and materialization (32) so near-parity small computations are still profitable when they eliminate a materialization/launch, with a looser 1.15 growth cap; cleanup stays at the manual 1.02. This tests the launch/materialization hypothesis directly against A/B's pure-work thresholds."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_91fd180c03424c2bb2873181","name":"submit_policy","arguments":{"policy_json":"{\"format\":\"kiln-transformation-program/1\",\"id\":\"sizeaware-exposure-a\",\"kind\":\"pointwise-fusion-sequence\",\"expose\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id\":\"expose-size-split\",\"kind\":\"selective\",\"tree\":{\"if\":{\"feature\":\"originalWork\",\"op\":\"lt\",\"value\":1000000},\"then\":{\"threshold\":0.9},\"else\":{\"threshold\":1.02}},\"consumer_tree\":{\"threshold\":1.02}},\"cleanup\":{\"format\":\"kiln-fusion-policy/1\",\"id